# code: (key ideas, common errors, calculator, VCAA phrasing)
TB={
"DA1":("Categorical (nominal: no order; ordinal: natural order) vs numerical (discrete: counted; continuous: measured). Numbers can be categorical (e.g. postcode, age group 1/2/3).","Calling numbers 'numerical' automatically; mixing up nominal and ordinal; counting variables wrongly in a table.","None needed.","Name the variable type exactly: 'nominal', 'ordinal', 'discrete numerical', 'continuous numerical'."),
"DA2":("Reading and building frequency tables, bar charts, histograms, dot/stem plots; describing shape (symmetric, positively/negatively skewed, outliers); log10 scale: a value of 3 means 10^3 = 1000.","Forgetting to convert log values back (10^x); reading the wrong column width on a histogram; using 'skewed left/right' instead of VCAA wording.","CAS log10 for conversions.","Describe shape using 'approximately symmetric', 'positively skewed', 'negatively skewed', and mention outliers."),
"DA3":("Median, quartiles, IQR = Q3 − Q1, range; boxplots; outliers are below Q1 − 1.5×IQR or above Q3 + 1.5×IQR; whiskers go to the most extreme non-outlier values.","Finding quartiles incorrectly for odd/even n; drawing whiskers to the fence instead of the last data value; forgetting to 'show' fence calculations.","CAS one-variable statistics gives Q1, median, Q3 (ClassPad: Statistics > One-Variable).","Show working: 'upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = …, and … > …, so it is an outlier.'"),
"DA4":("Mean and sample standard deviation; normal model 68–95–99.7% rule (use symmetry: 34%, 13.5%, 2.35%, 0.15%); z = (x − mean)/sd; comparing using z-scores.","Mixing up 2.5% vs 2.35%; not splitting the percentages symmetrically; rounding too early; confusing sd with IQR.","CAS for mean and sd (sample sd, sx).","Sketch a normal curve with mean ± 1, 2, 3 sd before answering."),
"DA5":("Explanatory vs response variable; two-way tables (percentage in the direction of the explanatory variable); segmented bar charts; parallel boxplots and back-to-back stem plots; describing associations by quoting percentages or medians.","Percentaging the table the wrong way; saying 'there is an association' without quoting specific percentages; swapping explanatory and response.","None usually needed.","'Yes, there is an association between … and … because x% of … compared with y% of …'"),
"DA6":("Correlation coefficient r: direction (sign), strength (weak/moderate/strong), form (linear); r = ±√r² (sign from slope); correlation does not imply causation.","Giving r² instead of r, or forgetting the negative sign; describing strength wrongly; concluding causation.","CAS linear regression gives r.","Describe association as 'strong, negative, linear'. For causation: 'correlation does not imply causation; other variables may be involved.'"),
"DA7":("Least squares line y = a + bx; interpreting slope ('on average, for each 1 unit increase in x, y increases/decreases by b units') and intercept; prediction; interpolation vs extrapolation; r² as '% of variation in response explained by variation in explanatory'.","Not referencing both variables; rounding to the wrong significant figures; putting variables the wrong way round; forgetting the words 'on average'.","CAS LinReg (a+bx): check which list is x and which is y.","Slope: 'On average, [response] increases by b [units] for each one [unit] increase in [explanatory].' r²: 'x% of the variation in [response] can be explained by the variation in [explanatory].'"),
"DA8":("Residual = actual − predicted; residual plot: random scatter means linear model appropriate, a clear pattern means non-linear; transformations (x², log x, 1/x, y², log y, 1/y) to linearise; predictions from a transformed equation.","Predicted − actual (wrong way round); forgetting to undo the transformation in predictions; misreading residual plot patterns.","CAS: create a transformed list, then LinReg on it.","'The residual plot shows no clear pattern, which supports the assumption of linearity.'"),
"DA9":("Time series features: trend, seasonality, cycles, structural change, irregular fluctuations, outliers; moving mean smoothing (odd and even with centring); median smoothing from a graph.","Forgetting to centre even-point smoothing; describing definitions instead of features seen in the plot; counting points wrongly.","CAS or by hand for moving means.","Name features VCAA-style: 'increasing trend with irregular fluctuations'."),
"DA10":("Seasonal index = actual ÷ seasonal average; indices sum to the number of seasons; deseasonalised = actual ÷ seasonal index; reseasonalise = deseasonalised × index; an index of 1.35 means 35% above average.","Multiplying instead of dividing when deseasonalising; % change to correct for seasonality (index 1.75 → reduce by 1 − 1/1.75 = 43%); averaging the wrong values.","By hand or CAS lists.","'The seasonal index of 0.75 means sales in summer are 25% below the seasonal average.'"),
"DA11":("Fitting a least squares trend line to (deseasonalised) time series; forecasting then reseasonalising; limitations of forecasting far into the future.","Forgetting to reseasonalise a forecast; using the wrong time number (e.g. month 1 vs 0).","CAS LinReg with time as x.","Mention extrapolation risk when forecasting far ahead."),
"RF1":("First-order linear recurrence u(n+1) = R·u(n) + d; generating terms; R > 1 growth, 0 < R < 1 decay, d the regular addition/subtraction; reading what u0, R and d represent.","Mixing up u0 and u1; using R = interest rate instead of 1 + r/100; wrong sign on d.","CAS: type u0, then ans×R + d, and press EXE repeatedly.","Always state u0 when writing a recurrence relation."),
"RF2":("Simple interest (linear growth: V(n+1) = V(n) + d); flat rate depreciation (V(n+1) = V(n) − d, where d = % of purchase price); unit cost depreciation (depreciation per unit × units used); rules V(n) = V0 ± n·d.","Taking the flat rate % of the current value instead of the purchase price; mixing up per km and per year.","None needed.","Recurrence: 'V0 = …, V(n+1) = V(n) − …'. Rule: 'V(n) = … − … × n'."),
"RF3":("Compound interest V(n+1) = R·V(n), R = 1 + r/100; reducing balance depreciation R = 1 − r/100; rule V(n) = R^n × V0; comparing with flat rate.","Using 1 + r instead of 1 + r/100; confusing reducing balance with flat rate; per-period rate errors.","Finance solver or solve(R^n × V0 = …).","Show R clearly, e.g. R = 0.82 for 18% reducing balance."),
"RF4":("Nominal (quoted annual) rate vs effective annual rate; r_eff = ((1 + r/(100n))^n − 1) × 100%; converting between compounding periods; comparing investments.","Using the formula with the rate as a percentage instead of a decimal; confusing which rate the question gives; forgetting CpY.","ClassPad/TI: finance app interest conversion (Nom/Eff).","Explain: 'the effective rate is higher because interest is compounded more than once a year.'"),
"RF5":("Reducing balance loans: recurrence V(n+1) = R·V(n) − d; amortisation tables (payment = interest + principal reduction); interest = balance × period rate; final payment adjustment; total interest paid.","Wrong period rate (annual not divided by 12/26/52); principal reduction = payment − interest mix-ups; rounding within tables.","Finance solver: N, I%, PV (+), Pmt (−), FV, PpY, CpY. Sign conventions matter.","'Interest decreases each payment because the balance is decreasing.'"),
"RF6":("Annuity: an invested balance paid out regularly (V(n+1) = R·V(n) − d); perpetuity: payment = interest only, so the balance never changes (d = V0 × r/100 per period); duration, final payment, interest rate.","Sign errors in the finance solver (PV negative for an investment); using an annual rate for monthly payments; confusing annuity with annuity investment.","Finance solver, especially solving for N or Pmt.","Perpetuity: 'the payment equals the interest earned each period, so the balance stays constant.'"),
"RF7":("Annuity investment: compound interest with regular additions V(n+1) = R·V(n) + d; future value; finding the payment, rate or time.","Sign conventions (PV and Pmt both negative, FV positive); forgetting the initial deposit.","Finance solver: PV and Pmt negative, FV positive.","State which finance solver values you used."),
"RF8":("Finance solver (TVM): N, I%, PV, Pmt, FV, PpY, CpY; money in = negative, money out = positive (from your perspective); multi-stage problems (balance after part of the loan, then re-solve).","Sign errors; wrong N (years × periods); forgetting to reset values between stages; reporting too many or too few decimal places.","ClassPad: Financial > Compound Interest. TI-Nspire: Finance Solver.","Write the solver inputs as working."),
"RF9":("Interest-only loan: each repayment equals the interest, so the balance stays constant; d = V0 × period rate; finding the rate, repayment or amount borrowed.","Using the annual rate for weekly/monthly periods; thinking the balance decreases.","Finance solver with FV = −PV, or by hand.","'The repayment equals the interest each period, so the balance does not change.'"),
"MA1":("Order (rows × columns); element a_ij; types (row, column, square, diagonal, identity, symmetric, triangular, binary, permutation); addition, scalar multiplication, products (inner dimensions must match); powers; transpose; building matrices from rules or tables; interpreting products in context.","Getting the product order wrong (AB ≠ BA); element notation mixed up (row first); not checking the order before multiplying; interpreting the elements of a product.","CAS matrix templates (ClassPad Math2 keyboard; TI templates).","State the order as 'm × n' and check defined products: (m × n)(n × p) → m × p."),
"MA2":("Determinant of 2×2: ad − bc; the inverse exists only if det ≠ 0; A × A⁻¹ = I; solving matrix equations AX = B → X = A⁻¹B (order matters).","Writing BA⁻¹ instead of A⁻¹B; sign errors in the 2×2 inverse; thinking every square matrix has an inverse.","CAS: det(), and ^−1.","'The inverse does not exist because the determinant is zero.'"),
"MA3":("Communication matrices (1 = direct link); two-step communication = M²; total = M + M²; dominance matrices (1 = row beats column); total dominance = D + D²; ranking.","Reading rows vs columns backwards; forgetting that a two-step path goes through another person; errors completing missing entries (each game has one winner).","CAS for D + D².","Explain an element in context: 'there are 2 ways for A to send a message to C through one other person.'"),
"MA4":("Transition diagrams ↔ transition matrices (columns = from/this state, rows = to/next state, columns sum to 1); interpreting elements; the leading diagonal = stay in the same state; regular transition matrices.","Putting 'from' in rows instead of columns; using percentages instead of decimals; columns not summing to 1.","CAS matrix entry.","'The element 0.36 means 36% of those in C this week are expected to be in G next week.'"),
"MA5":("State matrices S(n+1) = T·S(n), S(n) = T^n·S0; steady state (raise T to a large power); working backwards S(n) = T⁻¹·S(n+1); counting those who move between specific states over given weeks.","Off-by-one errors (week 1 = S0); multiplying in the wrong order (S·T); rounding intermediate states; forgetting to convert to percentages.","CAS: T^50 × S0 for steady state (ClassPad video: 'steady state matrix').","'In the long term, approximately x% will be in state A.'"),
"MA6":("S(n+1) = T·S(n) + B (restocking: + entries; culling: − entries); computing states step by step; choosing B to keep the total constant.","Applying T to B; forgetting B each step; using T^n shortcuts (not valid when B is present).","CAS: define T, S, B and iterate ans.","Show at least one step: 'S1 = T × S0 + B = …'."),
"MA7":("Leslie matrices: the first row holds birth rates (fecundity), the sub-diagonal holds survival rates; life-cycle diagrams; S(n+1) = L·S(n); long-term growth or decline.","Putting survival rates on the diagonal instead of the sub-diagonal; mixing up age groups; forgetting that only females are modelled.","CAS matrix powers.","'On average, 20% of females in age group 1 survive to age group 2.'"),
"ND1":("Vertices, edges, loops, degree (sum of degrees = 2 × edges); connected, simple, complete, bipartite, trees, bridges; isomorphic graphs; adjacency matrices (a loop counts 1; multiple edges counted).","Loops counted twice in the degree sum or once in the adjacency matrix; directed vs undirected mix-ups.","None needed.","Use the correct term: 'bridge', 'isolated vertex', 'degenerate', 'connected'."),
"ND2":("Planar graphs (can be redrawn with no crossing edges); Euler's formula v − e + f = 2 (VCAA: v + f = e + 2); counting faces including the outside face.","Forgetting the infinite outer face; applying Euler's formula to non-planar or disconnected graphs.","None needed.","Show the formula with the numbers substituted."),
"ND3":("Walk, trail (no repeated edges), path (no repeated vertices), circuit, cycle; Eulerian trail (exactly 2 odd vertices, start and end at them) and Eulerian circuit (all vertices even); Hamiltonian path/cycle (every vertex once); minimum distance with repeated edges.","Writing 'path' when 'Hamiltonian path' is needed; wrong start/end vertices for Eulerian trails; repeating the wrong edges.","None needed.","Name the route precisely: 'Eulerian circuit', 'Hamiltonian cycle'. Refer to vertex degrees when explaining."),
"ND4":("Trees (v − 1 edges); spanning trees; minimum spanning tree by inspection or Prim's algorithm; connector problems.","Creating a cycle; missing a vertex; adding the wrong weights.","None needed.","List the edges chosen in order when using Prim's."),
"ND5":("Directed flow networks; cut capacity (only count edges flowing source → sink across the cut); maximum flow = minimum cut; bottlenecks.","Counting edges that flow back across the cut; invalid cuts that don't separate source from sink.","None needed.","'The maximum flow equals the capacity of the minimum cut, which is …'"),
"ND6":("Shortest path by inspection or Dijkstra's algorithm; weighted graphs.","Stopping early; missing a shorter indirect route; giving a distance when a route is asked (or vice versa).","None needed.","Write the route as vertices: A – C – D – F."),
"ND7":("Matching/allocation problems; bipartite graphs; Hungarian algorithm (row reduce, column reduce, cover zeros with the minimum lines, adjust); maximising by subtracting from the largest value; non-square tables need a dummy row/column.","Stopping before the number of lines = n; covering zeros incorrectly; forgetting to go back to the original table to total the cost.","TI-Nspire Hungarian program (see MaffsGuru 2023 Exam 1 video description); ClassPad by hand.","Give the allocation and the total from the ORIGINAL table."),
"ND8":("Precedence tables ↔ activity networks; dummy activities; forward scan (EST = largest of predecessors' EST + duration); backward scan (LST); float = LST − EST; critical path (zero float); crashing (reduce critical activities at minimum cost, watch for new critical paths).","Taking the smallest instead of the largest in the forward scan; forgetting dummies; crashing a non-critical activity; missing that a second critical path appears.","None needed.","List the critical path as activities: A – C – E – J. For crashing, show the time and the cost."),
}
